/*
# Segmented

A row of mutually exclusive choices — the control you reach for when the options
are few, named, and worth showing all at once. Its choices are **data** — `items`, records like
`Menu`'s items and `DataGrid`'s columns:

```declare-fragment
Segmented [ value = { app.page },
    items = { [ ({ id: "controls", label: "Controls" }),
               ({ id: "table",    label: "Table" }),
               ({ id: "grid",     label: "DataGrid" }) ] },
    commit(v: string) { app.page = v }
    ]
```

### In a form

It is a value control: in a `Form` it takes `name`, `required` and `validate` as a text field
does, and a `Field` around it names it. Its value is the chosen segment's `id`.

### Tabs

A segmented control that switches what is shown below it — pages, panes — is a row of
tabs: use a `TabBar`, which is a `Segmented` in every other way and is heard as tabs.

### Why data, and not `Segment` children

Because the sliding highlight cannot be built any other way. The pill has to know
which choice is selected and how wide each one is, and a constraint may not ask
the view tree that: `childViews` carries **set membership only**, so `.length` is
live while anything reaching into the collection is refused —
`aggregation over a reactive node collection … derive from data [DECLARE7001]`.
The compiler names the answer.

### How wide

A `Segmented` is as wide as its labels need: give it no width and every label shows whole,
each with its margin (the theme's `segmentedLabelInset`) at either side. Its segments share
the width **equally** by default, each as wide as the widest label needs, which is the calm
form for a row of choices. With `segmentWidths = "proportional"`, each segment is as wide as
its own label instead.

Give it a `width` (or let a layout stretch it) when it should fill a space, such as a phone's
full-width switch. The width is shared out by the same rule, so extra room makes the segments
wider. When there is less room than the labels need, a label that doesn't fit ends in an
ellipsis inside its segment, keeping its margins, and a screen reader still says the whole
label: a label cut short is a bug to fix, and verify says so. No segment is narrower than `segmentMinWidth`.

### The pill

The highlight springs a **normalized lane** — a float index — not pixels. Its edge and width
are read off the segments at that index, so a resize recomputes them and the pill **snaps** to
the right place instead of skating across the bar on every layout change; between segments of
different widths it changes width as it slides. The chosen segment wears a raised `surface`
pill with a hairline.

```declare
App [ width = 340, height = 130, page: string = "one",
    Segmented [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 300, value = { app.page },
        items = { [ ({ id: "one", label: "One" }),
                   ({ id: "two", label: "Two" }),
                   ({ id: "three", label: "Three" }) ] },
        commit(v: string) { app.page = v }
        ],
    Text [ x = 20, y = 74, text = { "showing: " + app.page } ]
    ]
```


## fieldValue
The chosen segment's `id` — what a form judges and a `required` asks for.
## value
The active choice. Bind it both ways to your own state (`value <-> app.page`), or constrain it
and override `commit` to own the destination, or leave it alone and read `value` back — a `Segmented` works
standalone. Compared against each choice's `id` by identity, so any value type
works.

## _isSegmented
**Internal.** The marker a `SegmentedItem` reads to recognize its parent. A
`.declare` class cannot hold a reactive cell without declaring it, so a marker is
necessarily surface — that does not make it API.

## items
The segments, as **records**: `{ id, label }` or `{ id, icon, label }` — the same vocabulary
`Menu`'s items use. A choice shows a label **or** an icon, never both: with an icon, its
`label` is the segment's name to a screen reader. A choice may also
carry a `location`, which makes that segment a real link (see `SegmentedItem.location`).

## commit()
**Where the choice goes**, receiving the picked `id`. The default writes `value`;
override it to own the destination.

## pick()
Selects a choice by id, routing through `commit`. The seam a segment calls.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole control — track, pill and every segment alike, never a
recolour — to the theme's `disabledOpacity`, or 0.45 when the theme states none.
**A segment is not separately disable-able**: a disabled group leaves the tab order
entirely rather than offering unreachable stops.

## segmentWidths
How the segments share the control's width: `equal` (the default), every segment as wide as
the widest label needs; or `proportional`, each as wide as its own label needs. Either way a
`width` you give the control is shared out by the same rule.

## segmentMinWidth
The narrowest a segment may be, however short its label: 44 by default, a finger's width, so
a one-letter choice is still easy to hit.

## _count
**Internal.** How many items there are, floored at 1 so the width arithmetic never divides by zero.

## _inset
**Internal.** A label's margin at each side of its segment, from the theme's
`segmentedLabelInset`.

## _needs
**Internal.** What each segment needs: its label measured in the face the segments draw it in
(or its icon's size), plus `_inset` at each side, and at least `segmentMinWidth`. Measured
from the records, as `Menu` sizes itself: a constraint may not gather its children's sizes.

## _wants
**Internal.** What each segment asks for, by `segmentWidths`: the widest need, or its own.

## _total
**Internal.** What the segments ask for together.

## _widths
**Internal.** Each segment's width: the control's width, less the track's inset, shared out
in proportion to what each asks.

## _lefts
**Internal.** Each segment's left edge, and one more, where the last ends.

## _at()
**Internal.** A value along the row at a fractional index, blending between neighboring
segments: where the pill's edge is, and how wide it is, as it slides.

## _idx
**Internal.** Which item is live, as an index into `items`, from the **data** — the only place the
question can be asked. `−1` (nothing selected) parks the pill at 0 and hides it rather
than springing to a phantom slot.

## _lane
**Internal.** The pill's position as a **float index**, sprung toward `_idx`. The whole reason the pill
is correct at any size: its edge and width are `_at(_lefts, _lane)` and `_at(_widths, _lane)`, so a resize recomputes them and the pill **snaps** to the right place instead of skating across the
bar on every layout change.

## segs()
The segment views, for the Tab entry. A handler-only walk — a constraint may not
aggregate the view tree.

## focusScope
`stop`: the control is one Tab stop — Tab lands on the selected segment and the next
Tab leaves; the arrows move among the segments, wrapping, as in any stop. Space or Enter
picks where they land, so **an inactive choice is reachable and selectable entirely from
the keyboard**, and nothing changes until it is asked to — a choice that is a place is
followed on Enter, as a link is, not on the arrow.

## focusEntry()
The segment Tab lands on: the selected one, else the first.

## _rad
**Internal.** The corner radius from the theme's `segmentedCornerRadius`, which the pill and each hover sit 2 inside.

## fill
The track: the theme's `trackFill`, else `control`. The pill is `segmentedSelectedFill`, else
`surface`, and the label on it `segmentedSelectedTextColor`, else `text` — a theme that makes the
pill dark gives it a light label beside it. Where the theme gives `segmentDividerColor` (none by default), a divider stands
between segments; one beside the pill fades as the pill reaches it, so they part around the
selection as it slides.

## width
What the segments ask for (`_total`, and the track's inset), unless you give one; then it is
shared out among them by `segmentWidths`, and a label with too little room ends in an
ellipsis inside its segment.

## datapath
Roots the control on its own data, so the segments replicate from `items` without the author writing a datapath.

## height
From the theme's `segmentedHeight` (30 by default); the labels' size is `segmentedFontSize` (13).

## cornerRadius
The control's `_rad`.
*/

/*
# SegmentedItem

One choice of a `Segmented`, **materialized from that control's `items` data — never
authored directly**, which is why it carries almost nothing of its own. It is a full
`Control`: its own tab stop, activated by Space or Enter, so a user can tab to an
inactive choice and select it.

It renders rest, hover and press only. **The chosen state is the sliding pill behind
these, not a fill of its own** — which is exactly what lets the pill travel between
segments instead of popping.

## on
Whether this segment is the group's current choice, derived from the parent by identity.

## ink
This segment's text and icon colour, brightening when it is on or hovered. One attribute, so a
label and an icon can never disagree.

## location
Makes this segment a **real link**: give a choice a `location` and the segment declares
`link = "#…"` to it, so it renders as a real anchor the crawler follows, ⌘-click opens,
and a click or Enter navigates. A view-switching control is often the only edge into
half of an app, so the switch has to say where it goes. Empty for an ordinary segmented
control, and an empty location is no link at all.

## link
`"#"` + this segment's `location`, or empty — the segment is a link exactly when its
choice names a place, and never while the group is disabled.

## replace
The group's own `replace`: whether following this segment's link overwrites the history
entry instead of pushing one.

## press()
Delivers this segment's id to the group.

## onClick
Presses. A segment whose choice carries a `location` also follows its `link`.

## onKeyDown
Space and Enter pick; the arrows move among the segments (the stop's).

## focusShape()
Rings the segment's pill inset, not its whole box.

## _isSegment
**Internal.** The marker a `Segmented` reads to find its own segments — not API.

## height
As tall as the control.

## width
Its share of the control's width, by the control's `segmentWidths` (`_widths`).

## x
Placed by index: its left edge in the control's `_lefts`.

## disabled
Disabled with the control; an item never disables alone.

## accessibilityChecked
Follows `on`: a segment is one radio of its group.

## accessibilityLabel
The record's whole `label`, whether the segment shows it, shows an icon, or shows it cut short.
*/

include [ "valuecontrol.declare", "icons/icon.declare" ]

class Segmented extends ValueControl [
    // one choice among a few: a group of radios to a screen reader, whatever it
    // looks like (a page switcher, an appearance switch)

    accessibilityRole = radioGroup,

    // the marker a SegmentedItem reads to recognize its parent

    _isSegmented: boolean = true,

    items: array = [],             // a bare literal: it owns a cell, so a use-site value wires
    value: object = null,

    // a form's member: its value is the chosen segment's id, under its `name`

    fieldValue = { value },

    // one Tab stop, landing on the selected segment; the arrows move inside
    // (planes.md §13.6)

    focusScope = stop,
    focusable = false,             // its segments are the stops, not the group
    focusEntry() -> View? {
        const ss = this.segs()
        return (ss[Math.max(0, this._idx)] ?? null) as View | null
        },

    // The group honors View's own `replace`: with `replace = true`, a pick of
    // a choice that carries a `location` replaces the history entry instead
    // of pushing one — the viewer's Reader/Source/Edit, where Back should
    // return to the previous place, never the previous tab. Default false: a
    // switch that is itself navigation (the docs' Guide/Reference) keeps
    // pushing.

    height = { provided("theme").segmentedHeight },
    _rad: number = { provided("theme").segmentedCornerRadius },
    cornerRadius = { this._rad },
    fill = { provided("theme").trackFill ?? provided("theme").control },
    opacity = { disabled ? (provided("theme").disabledOpacity) : 1 },

    // ── the widths
    //
    // A segment needs its label (or icon) plus the label's margin at each side,
    // and never less than `segmentMinWidth`. The labels are measured from the
    // records, in the face the segments draw them in: a fold over data, as
    // Menu sizes itself, since a constraint may not gather the children's sizes.
    // `equal` asks the widest need for every segment; `proportional`, each its
    // own. The control's width is shared out in proportion to what they ask, so
    // a given width is honored, and a label with too little room ends in an
    // ellipsis inside its margins.

    segmentWidths: "equal" | "proportional" = "equal",
    segmentMinWidth: number = 44,  // a segment is never narrower: a finger's width
    _count: number = { Math.max(1, ((this.items ?? []) as any).length) },
    _inset: number = { provided("theme").segmentedLabelInset },
    _needs: array = { ((this.items ?? []) as any).map((r) => Math.max(this.segmentMinWidth, 2 * this._inset + Math.ceil(r.icon != null
        ? provided("iconSize", 16)
        : measureText("" + (r.label ?? ""), providedTextStyle(({ fontSize: provided("theme").segmentedFontSize, fontWeight: "medium" }))).width))) },
    _wants: array = { this.segmentWidths == "equal" ? this._needs.map(() => Math.max(...this._needs)) : this._needs },
    _total: number = { this._wants.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) },
    width  = { Math.max(this._total, this.segmentMinWidth) + 4 },

    // each segment's width and left edge (one more, the end), from the width the control has

    _widths: array = { this._wants.map((w) => w * (this.width - 4) / Math.max(1, this._total)) },
    _lefts: array = { this._widths.reduce((a, w) => [...a, a[a.length - 1] + w], [2]) },

    // a value along the row at a fractional index: the pill's edge and width as it slides

    _at(a: array, lane: number) -> number {
        const i = Math.max(0, Math.min(a.length - 1, Math.floor(lane)))
        return a[i] + ((a[i + 1] ?? a[i]) - a[i]) * (lane - i)
        },

    // Which choice is live, as an index — from the data, which is the only place
    // this question can be asked. −1 (nothing selected) parks the pill at 0 and
    // hides it rather than springing to a phantom slot.

    _idx: number = { ((this.items ?? []) as any).findIndex((c) => c.id === this.value) },

    // ── the delivery seam ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    commit(v: object) { value = v },
    pick(v: object) { edit(v) },          // a person's pick: through edit, so a form judges it

    // the member walk — handlers only; a constraint may not aggregate the tree

    segs() -> array {
        const out = []
        for (const c of this.children) { if ((c as SegmentedItem)._isSegment === true) out.push(c) }
        return out
        },

    // The pill. `_lane` is a float index the spring drives; the pill's edge and
    // width are read off the segments at that index, so the pill is correct at
    // any size the moment layout changes and only animates when the selection
    // does: between segments of different widths it changes width as it goes.

    _lane: number = 0,
    Spring [ attribute = _lane, to = { classroot._idx < 0 ? 0 : classroot._idx },
        stiffness = 260, damping = 30, mass = 0.9, epsilon = 0.002 ],

    _pill: View [ y = 2, height = { parent.height - 4 }, cornerRadius = { Math.max(0, (parent as Segmented)._rad - 2) },
        x = { (parent as Segmented)._at((parent as Segmented)._lefts, (parent as Segmented)._lane) },
        width = { (parent as Segmented)._at((parent as Segmented)._widths, (parent as Segmented)._lane) },
        visible = { (parent as Segmented)._idx >= 0 },
        fill = { provided("theme").segmentedSelectedFill ?? provided("theme").surface },
        stroke = { Stroke(1, provided("theme").line) } ],

    // Dividers between segments, where the theme gives them a colour
    // (`segmentDividerColor`; none by default). One beside the pill fades as
    // the pill reaches it, so they part around the selection as it slides.

    _rules: View [ width = 100%, height = 100%, pointerEvents = "none",
        visible = { provided("theme").segmentDividerColor != null },
        draw(d: DrawContext) {
            const s = parent as Segmented
            d.fillStyle = (provided("theme").segmentDividerColor ?? provided("theme").line)
            for (let i = 1; i < s._count; i++) {
                const near = s._idx < 0 ? 1 : Math.abs(s._lane + 0.5 - i)
                const a = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (near - 0.5) * 2))
                if (a <= 0) continue
                d.globalAlpha = a
                d.fillRect((s._lefts[i] ?? 0) - 0.5, 7, 1, this.height - 14)
                }
            }
        ],

    // The index rides in the data — replication has no ordinal of its own, and
    // the pill needs one to place each segment.

    _segmentRows: Dataset [ contents = { { rows: ((classroot.items ?? []) as any).map((c, i) => ({ ...c, i: i })) } } ],

    // Replicated as direct children, so a segment's `parent` is the control
    // itself — the pill and the segments are siblings over one cursor.

    datapath = { this._segmentRows.value },
    SegmentedItem [ datapath = :rows[] ]
    ]


// One choice. A Control — its own tab stop, Space/Enter activating the pick, so
// the user tabs to an inactive choice and selects it. Materialized from the
// data; not authored directly, which is why it carries no attributes of its own
// beyond what the record gives it.

class SegmentedItem extends Control [
    _isSegment: boolean = true,

    height = 100%,
    width  = { ((parent as Segmented)._widths ?? [])[:i ?? 0] ?? 0 },
    x      = { ((parent as Segmented)._lefts ?? [])[:i ?? 0] ?? 2 },

    on: boolean = { (parent as Segmented).value === :id },
    accessibilityRole = radio,
    accessibilityChecked = { on },

    // a segment is named by its record's whole label, whether it shows the label, an
    // icon, or a label cut short by the room it has

    accessibilityLabel = { "" + (:label ?? "") },

    // Rest, hover and press only — the chosen state is the sliding pill behind
    // these, not a fill of their own. That is what lets it travel between them.

    _bg: View [ x = 2, y = 2, width = { parent.width - 4 }, height = { parent.height - 4 },
        cornerRadius = { Math.max(0, (parent.parent as Segmented)._rad - 2) },
        stateFill: Color? = { parent.pressed ? provided("theme").controlPressed : parent.hovered ? provided("theme").controlHovered : null },
        fill = { parent.on ? null : this.stateFill }
        ],

    // A choice shows a label or an icon, whichever the record carries — page
    // switches are labelled, appearance switches are the sun/half-disc/moon —
    // never both. The record shape is `Menu`'s.

    // on the pill, the pill's own text colour (a dark pill takes a light label), as
    // accent / accentTextColor pair; else the theme's text

    ink: Color = { this.on ? provided("theme").segmentedSelectedTextColor ?? provided("theme").text
        : this.hovered ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").textMuted },

    // the label keeps its margin at each side, and ends in an ellipsis when that
    // leaves too little room — it never runs past its segment

    _t: TextLabel [ wrap = false, maxLines = 1, fontSize = { provided("theme").segmentedFontSize }, fontWeight = medium,
        x = { (parent.parent as Segmented)._inset ?? 0 }, width = { Math.max(0, parent.width - 2 * ((parent.parent as Segmented)._inset ?? 0)) },
        textAlign = center,
        visible = { :label != null && :icon == null },
        textColor = { parent.ink },
        text = { "" + (:label ?? "") } ],

    _ico: IconHost [ kind = { "" + (:icon ?? "") }, ink = { parent.ink }, x = center, y = center ],

    // The control's own disabled flag follows the group's — a segment is not
    // separately disable-able, and a disabled group takes itself out of the tab
    // order rather than offering unreachable stops.

    disabled = { (parent as Segmented).disabled },

    // A segment may be a location. Give a choice a `location` and this segment
    // is a link to it — a real anchor the crawler follows, not a handler that
    // navigates. A view-switching control is often the only edge into half of
    // the crawl graph (the docs' Guide/Reference switch), so the switch itself
    // says where it goes. The group's `replace` picks the history verb. Empty
    // for an ordinary segmented control: an empty link is no link.

    location: string = { "" + (:location ?? "") },
    link = { !disabled && classroot.location != "" ? "#" + classroot.location : "" },
    replace = { (parent as Segmented).replace },

    press()   { if (!disabled) (this.parent as Segmented).pick(:id) },
    onClick() { press() },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") { press(); return }
        super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    // the ring hugs the segment's pill, not its whole box

    focusShape() { return { x: 2, y: 2, w: this.width - 4, h: this.height - 4, rad: Math.max(0, (this.parent as Segmented)._rad - 2) } }
    ]
